1
00:00:00,594 --> 00:00:03,501
Of course, you can use Ollama through that

2
00:00:03,629 --> 00:00:05,862
command line based chat interface.

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,283
Or as briefly shown earlier, you could use

4
00:00:09,283 --> 00:00:11,692
it in conjunction with a solution like Open

5
00:00:11,757 --> 00:00:15,098
WebUI to add a graphical user interface to

6
00:00:15,114 --> 00:00:15,178
it.

7
00:00:15,852 --> 00:00:17,571
Though if you want one, as explained before,

8
00:00:18,005 --> 00:00:20,510
my recommendation would be to just go for LM

9
00:00:20,526 --> 00:00:23,402
Studio. But you can also use Ollama

10
00:00:23,402 --> 00:00:24,462
programmatically,

11
00:00:24,992 --> 00:00:27,272
just as you could use LM Studio

12
00:00:27,417 --> 00:00:28,349
programmatically.

13
00:00:28,943 --> 00:00:30,902
And you can therefore take advantage of all

14
00:00:31,110 --> 00:00:34,001
the benefits Ollama gives you, like the

15
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:36,939
ability to easily build your own customized

16
00:00:37,051 --> 00:00:39,781
model versions and use those so that you

17
00:00:39,845 --> 00:00:42,270
could have different models that have

18
00:00:42,270 --> 00:00:44,437
specific system prompts for different

19
00:00:44,437 --> 00:00:46,252
situations and you could easily switch

20
00:00:46,284 --> 00:00:46,733
between them.

21
00:00:47,392 --> 00:00:50,025
You can use all that programmatically as

22
00:00:50,137 --> 00:00:50,282
well

23
00:00:51,165 --> 00:00:55,452
because Ollama, like LM Studio, also runs a

24
00:00:55,452 --> 00:00:57,893
server behind the scenes, a server to which

25
00:00:57,925 --> 00:01:00,992
you can send requests from inside your code.

26
00:01:01,538 --> 00:01:04,476
Now, unlike with LM Studio, you don't need

27
00:01:04,509 --> 00:01:08,250
to explicitly enable that server. Instead,

28
00:01:08,362 --> 00:01:10,755
it's started automatically when you start

29
00:01:10,835 --> 00:01:11,333
Ollama.

30
00:01:12,039 --> 00:01:14,512
Now, in case it stopped for whatever reason,

31
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:17,643
for example, you quit Ollama through the

32
00:01:17,676 --> 00:01:21,706
Windows system tray or the macOS status bar

33
00:01:21,754 --> 00:01:23,905
here, If that happened, you can always run

34
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:25,959
ollama serve to bring it back up.

35
00:01:26,729 --> 00:01:29,040
If you run that command, by the way, you

36
00:01:29,216 --> 00:01:31,479
also get a detailed output of the

37
00:01:31,639 --> 00:01:34,672
configuration that was set for that server,

38
00:01:35,410 --> 00:01:37,705
like for example, the default context length

39
00:01:37,881 --> 00:01:38,523
that's being set.

40
00:01:39,165 --> 00:01:41,588
And you're also stuck in a running process

41
00:01:41,732 --> 00:01:44,043
where you will see server log messages.

42
00:01:44,797 --> 00:01:46,675
If you don't want that, you can quit this

43
00:01:47,365 --> 00:01:47,974
and you can simply

44
00:01:48,632 --> 00:01:49,419
ollama run

45
00:01:50,013 --> 00:01:50,526
a model

46
00:01:51,442 --> 00:01:53,208
and then quit, for example, and this will

47
00:01:53,369 --> 00:01:55,280
also start that server again.

48
00:01:56,035 --> 00:01:58,589
So now Ollama is also running again here and

49
00:01:58,669 --> 00:02:01,030
I would have to quit it again if I wanted to

50
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:02,989
get rid of that background process.

51
00:02:03,969 --> 00:02:05,992
And with that server up and running, you can

52
00:02:05,992 --> 00:02:08,305
communicate with it from inside your code

53
00:02:08,321 --> 00:02:09,927
and that's what we'll explore next.
